sbleas@chiark.greenend.org.uk (Sebastian Bleasdale) writes:
> One possibility is that if you don't do things for some time, then
> you start to get worse at them.  You want to bash something with your
> sword - fine, but if you haven't done any serious fighting for the last
> 10000 turns, then you won't be particularly good at it.

An interesting idea, but from my experiences playing/running/designing
pen-and-paper RPGs, I will caution you that:

   1) We still don't know a whole lot of hard facts about the details
      of how people learn and retain complex skills in real life.

   2) Building a good model of what little we do know doesn't
      necessarily make your game more fun to play, or improve WSoD, or
      give you a glossy coat -- even if you have a computer taking
      care of the bookkeeping chores.

OTOH, this sort of thing could make for complex, emergent behavior,
and that sounds a lot more nethack-ish than the "let's make the game
act as though you'd typed '.' if you go more than five seconds without
pressing a key" sorts of ideas.

